ad as a weighted share of disclosed itemized money.","donorProfileCycle":2026,"billClassificationMethod":"llm","unclassifiedDonorShare":0.5315961335553588},"bill":{"id":"119-hr-1722","congress":119,"billType":"hr","billNumber":"1722","title":"Billion Dollar Boondoggle Act","introducedDate":"2025-02-27","latestActionDate":"2026-07-23","latestActionText":"Received in the Senate. Read twice. Placed on Senate Legislative Calendar under General Orders. Calendar No. 467.","policyArea":"Government Operations and Politics","subjects":["Congressional oversight","Government information and archives","Public contracts and procurement"],"sponsorBioguideId":"M001215","cosponsorCount":3,"committeeNames":[],"congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/1722","industries":[{"industry":"defense","confidence":0.85,"rationale":"Defense contractors frequently work on large federal projects that may trigger the reporting thresholds for schedule delays or cost overruns."},{"industry":"construction","confidence":0.75,"rationale":"Construction and engineering firms execute major federal infrastructure and building projects that could exceed cost estimates or timelines."},{"industry":"tech","confidence":0.65,"rationale":"Technology contractors working on large federal IT modernization and systems projects may face increased reporting obligations if projects run over budget or schedule."}],"plainSummary":"This bill requires the Office of Management and Budget to collect and publish an annual report on federal projects that are either more than five years behind schedule or have gone at least $1 billion over their original cost estimate. Agencies must explain the reasons for delays and cost overruns, describe changes to project scope, and disclose any bonuses or incentive fees paid. The report must be submitted to Congress and posted online.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":{"whoItTouches":"Anyone who deals with a federal agency.","confidence":"crs-summary"